   "id": "76A9--irBgAJ",
   "from": "Johan Zvrskovec",
   "author": "ba1d5380065b224f",
   "date": "2021-10-11T15:32:33Z",
   "subject": "Retaining SNPs in sumstats",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi,<div>Do you have suggestions for maximising the number of SNP&#39;s retained by the sumstats() function? It seems to only keep the intersect of variants across all indicator variable datasets. Is there an easy way to impute missing values, maybe using the previously fitted latent factor structure?</div><div><br></div><div>Kind regards</div><div>Johan</div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "0mXlqauuBgAJ",
   "from": "Elliot Tucker-Drob",
   "author": "ac421cf36d1a4c37",
   "date": "2021-10-11T16:23:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: Retaining SNPs in sumstats",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div><div dir=\"auto\">You would need to impute each sample to the same reference panel and redo the Univariate GWASs… </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">Otherwise you are in the same territory that is common for cross-referencing hits across GWASs where you might do something like find a proxy SNP for a variant of interest that isn’t genotypes or imputed..  </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div><div dir=\"auto\">I can think of more creative solutions off the top of my head but we haven’t tested or implemented those. </div><div dir=\"auto\"><br></div></div><div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Oct 11, 2021 at 10:32 AM &#39;Johan Zvrskovec&#39; via Genomic SEM Users &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">genomic-...@googlegroups.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204)\">Hi,<div>Do you have suggestions for maximising the number of SNP&#39;s retained by the sumstats() function? It seems to only keep the intersect of variants across all indicator variable datasets. Is there an easy way to impute missing values, maybe using the previously fitted latent factor structure?</div><div><br></div><div>Kind regards</div><div>Johan</div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div></div></div>"
    ],
